10:22:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Your body does use glucose for energy, but not all food becomes glucose, and foods affect the body in very different ways before, during, and after digestion. A few key points: - **Carbohydrates** are most directly broken down into glucose. But a sugary drink enters the blood very quickly, while beans or oats release glucose slowly because they contain fiber and take longer to digest. - **Fats** are mostly broken into fatty acids, which many tissues can burn directly for energy. They can also be stored efficiently as body fat. - **Protein** is broken into amino acids, mainly used to build and repair muscle, organs, enzymes, hormones, and immune cells. The body *can* turn some amino acids into glucose, but that is not protein’s main job. - **Fiber** is not turned into glucose at all. It supports digestion, gut bacteria, fullness, cholesterol control, and steadier blood sugar. - **Vitamins and minerals** do not provide calories, but they are necessary for the chemical reactions that let your body use energy, build tissue, carry oxygen, and regulate nerves and hormones. Even when two foods ultimately provide the same number of calories or grams of glucose, they can differ in: - how fast they raise blood sugar and insulin - how full you feel - whether they provide protein, fiber, and micronutrients - effects on cholesterol, gut health, muscle maintenance, and long-term disease risk For example, 200 calories of soda and 200 calories of lentils may eventually contribute energy, but soda is mostly rapidly absorbed sugar, while lentils bring slow-digesting carbohydrates, protein, fiber, iron, potassium, and other nutrients. So glucose is one important fuel, but food is also the body’s raw material, maintenance supply, and signaling system—not just gasoline.
